 in principle to from squatting  even though the load is slightly less  because the relative length of that  moment arm is quite a bit longer that  still means even with a lower weight  you're presenting a larger relative  challenge to those thoracic erectors to  keep the spine extended so you can  transfer that force from your legs and  hips into the bar so that's the primary  reason once you look at everything it's  not your quads it's certainly not your  hip extensors because they're having to  work harder for low bar and I think we  can safely assume it's not your calves  so it has to be well and it's not those  it's probably not those lumbar extensors  either because when you look at the  total torque they have to produce even  though the moment arm is longer for high  bar  that's probably canceled out by the  slightly less amount of weight so torque  is probably similar however there's a  pretty sizable difference in the  thoracic spine so there's probably where  our weakness is coming from so back  strength is the main factor specifically  thoracic erector strength however  there's something else that plays into  this as well I'll be it to a slightly  lesser degree and that is where the  bottom position is so with low bar squat  since you're in a few greater degrees of  hip flexion with any  saluté amount of dorsiflexion in  deflection what that's going to mean is  you simply hit the bottom position of a  squat you reach that full hip flexion  position a little bit sooner and that  means essentially that when you're  getting that rebound that stretch reflex  that it is going to help carry you  through a more relevant portion of the  lift what you see here is based on  research the average sticking point of  the squat slightly above parallel femur  at about a 30 degree angle to the floor  so when you're doing slightly deeper  high bar squats you catch that stretch  reflex you're quite a bit below parallel  it helps you get that bounce but then  it's largely dissipated by the time  you're even nearing that sticking point  the biggest portion of the lift  however for low bar squats since you're  in a few greater degrees of hip flexion  you hit that bottom position a little  bit higher that stretch reflex helps  carry you through a somewhat more  relevant portion of the lift so you may  still have some speed and power from  that stretch reflex still hanging around  as you start approaching that sticking  point meaning that you may have a little  bit more speed going into the sticking  point to help you carry through it  instead of having to get through it  mainly on just pure horsepower the same  way you would have to for a high bar  squat that is the major reason why a lot  of weight lifters who have turned a  power lifter do tend to squat a little  bit more low bar that high bar however  the spread isn't nearly as large as it  is for power lifters for power lifters  about a 10 percent spread is pretty  common  however for weight lifters that's quite  a bit smaller and the main reason for  that is remember the primary limiting  factor is thoracic erector strength  weightlifting training you're doing a  ton of pulls you're doing a ton of front  squats a lot of things that really put a  premium on upper back strength so  they're less likely to be limited by  that the  a powerlifter is however where that  bottom position is having a slightly  higher natural bottom position because  most people once they've been under the  bar for a while  realize that if you can sink the squat a  couple inches deeper and catch that  stretch reflex you tend to be stronger  than cutting it a couple inches higher  not necessarily a couple inches above  parallel but you know let's say you can  you can squat three inches below  parallel and that's your bottom position  where you catch that stretch reflex  you'll tend to be a little bit stronger  squatting all the way down there than  you would if you could it's a one-inch  below parallel so that's still in play  so a lot of weight lifters when they  make the switch over to powerlifting max  Aida and chin way lean are two great  examples of this they've moved over to  low bar squatting for powerlifting  because it is still somewhat  advantageous  however the spread between their high  bar and low bar squats isn't nearly as  great as it would be for someone who's  only been a power lifter because since  they have that weightlifting background  they're not as limited by that thoracic  extensor strength so in wrapping up  those are the two main reasons why you  can squat more low bar than high bar in  spite of the fact that low bar has the  same knee extension demands in even  greater hip extension demands the first  thing is you're less likely to be  limited by spinal erectors strength  specifically thoracic erectors and the  second thing is that stretch reflex  tends to help you out through a more  relevant portion of the lift closer to  parallel and through the sticking point  now just in conclusion one of the pieces  of pushback that I'm expecting to give  is someone to post a picture similar to  this one of someone doing a low bar  squat with a super upright bottom  position and say oh no one who low bar  squats looks like that no one's that  bright you're trying to say that these  are two quite similar movements with  just a couple small differences between  them but see look at this picture of  someone with beautiful bottom position  of a high bar squat  lobar squad will never look like that  this is complete nonsense however  remember the most relevant portion of  the lift when we're talking about  mechanics and what's likely to be the  limiting factor is where you miss the  lift which tends to be somewhere around  parallel to just above parallel through  that sticking point and when you compare  even someone who has a really a bright  super deep bottom position for a high  bar squat like this when they start  coming up out of the hole their hips  tend to kick back and they wind up in a  position that looks much much more  similar to the one we're talking about  and if you'll notice there's quite a bit  of forward lean still it looks well like  a low bar squat except the bar a couple  inches up higher and conversely a low  bar squat through the sticking point  unless it's someone who has just a super  super hip dominant squat tends to look  like a high bar squat with the bar two  or three inches lower and that's what  you would expect because bar position  doesn't really change that much even  though people like to make a big deal  about it alright guys so that's all I've  got for this video I'm sure you are  starting to get tired of hearing me  rambling but if this is a subject that  interests you check out the description  there will be four or five articles down  there that I've written about this topic  that go into quite a bit more depth if  you'd like to deepen your understanding  of this specific topic but also just  squatting mechanics in general as always  if you liked the video please like and  subscribe and thanks a lot for watching

